[[File:Challenge of Amaterashu and Susanoo.svg|thumb|right|240px|The "Ukei" (Divine Oath) between {{ill|Amaterasu|ja|アマテラス|lt=Amaterasu}} and {{ill|Susanoo|ja|スサノヲ|lt=Susanoo}} (based on the ''{{ill|Kojiki|ja|古事記}}'')]]

'''Kotozaki Hachiman-gū''' (琴崎八幡宮, Kotozaki Hachiman-gū) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|ja|神社}} located in {{ill|Ube, Yamaguchi|ja|宇部市|lt=Ube City}}, {{ill|Yamaguchi Prefecture|ja|山口県|lt=Yamaguchi Prefecture}}, Japan. It was formerly a {{ill|Prefectural shrine|ja|県社}} and is currently a {{ill|Beppyo Shrine|ja|別表神社}} under the {{ill|Association of Shinto Shrines|ja|神社本庁|lt=Jinja Honchō}}. It is revered as the general tutelary shrine (総鎮守, sōchinju) of Ube City.

The shrine enshrines the {{ill|Hachiman|ja|八幡神}}—{{ill|Hajime Wakikimi|ja|品陀和気命}}, {{ill|Kichoushihime|ja|気長足比女命}}, and {{ill|Takanakatsuhiko|ja|足仲津比古命}}—as its main deities, along with the {{ill|Munakata Sanjoshin|ja|宗像三女神}} (Munakata Three Goddesses) {{ill|Takiri-hime|ja|多紀理比女命}}, {{ill|Tagitsu-hime|ja|多紀津比女命}}, and {{ill|Ichikishima-hime|ja|市杵島比女命}} as subsidiary deities.

== History ==
According to shrine tradition, in 859 ({{ill|Jōgan era|ja|貞観 (日本)|lt=Jōgan}}), {{ill|Gyōkyō|ja|行教}} carried the Hachiman deity from {{ill|Usa Jingū|ja|宇佐神宮|lt=Usa Jingū}} to {{ill|Iwashimizu Hachimangū|ja|石清水八幡宮|lt=Iwashimizu Hachimangū}}. During this journey, a temporary lodging (行宮, angū) was established at Kotetsu-no-ura in Ubegō (宇部郷琴芝浦), and after departing, the villagers enshrined a divided spirit of Hachiman at the site, marking the origin of Kotozaki Hachiman-gū. In 1377 ({{ill|Tenshō era|ja|天授 (日本)|lt=Tenshō}} 3), following a divine oracle, the shrine was relocated to its current location at Kotozaki. It has been revered by successive ruling clans, including the {{ill|Atsuto clan|ja|厚東氏}}, {{ill|Ōuchi clan|ja|大内氏}}, and {{ill|Mōri clan|ja|毛利氏}}.

In 1873 ({{ill|Meiji era|ja|明治}} 6), the shrine was ranked as a {{ill|Gōsha|ja|郷社}}, and in 1938 ({{ill|Shōwa era|ja|昭和}} 13), it was elevated to a {{ill|Prefectural shrine|ja|県社}}. In July 1966 ({{ill|Shōwa era|ja|昭和}} 41), it was included among the {{ill|Beppyo Shrines|ja|別表神社}} of the {{ill|Association of Shinto Shrines|ja|神社本庁|lt=Jinja Honchō}}.

== Deities Enshrined ==
* **Primary Deities**:
  * {{ill|Hajime Wakikimi|ja|品陀和気命|lt=Hajime Wakikimi}}
  * {{ill|Kichoushihime|ja|気長足比女命|lt=Kichoushihime}}
  * {{ill|Takanakatsuhiko|ja|足仲津比古命|lt=Takanakatsuhiko}}
* **Subsidiary Deities**:
  * {{ill|Takiri-hime|ja|多紀理比女命|lt=Takiri-hime}}
  * {{ill|Tagitsu-hime|ja|多紀津比女命|lt=Tagitsu-hime}}
  * {{ill|Ichikishima-hime|ja|市杵島比女命|lt=Ichikishima-hime}}

== Grounds ==
* **Honden (Main Hall)** - Rebuilt on March 29, 1992 ({{ill|Heisei era|ja|平成}} 4)
* **Norito-den (Hall of Blessing)** - Rebuilt in 1992 ({{ill|Heisei era|ja|平成}} 4)
* **Haiden (Worship Hall)** - Rebuilt in 1992 ({{ill|Heisei era|ja|平成}} 4)
* **Shamusho (Office)**
* **Shinsenjo (Food Offering Hall)**
* **Saikiko (Ceremonial Instrument Storehouse)**
* **Monuments**:
  * {{ill|61st Infantry Regiment|ja|歩兵第六十一聯隊}} Cenotaph
  * {{ill|218th Infantry Regiment|ja|歩兵第二百十八聯隊}} Memorial
  * {{ill|230th Infantry Regiment|ja|歩兵第二百三十聯隊}} Soul Monument
  * {{ill|Manshū-Mō Kaitaku Seishonen Giyūgun|ja|満蒙開拓青少年義勇軍}} Commemoration Monument
* **Round Table and Chairs** - Made of concrete. Originally located under the wisteria trellis in the canteen of the {{ill|61st Infantry Regiment|ja|歩兵第六十一聯隊}}.

== Festivals ==
* **Annual Festival** - October 15
* **Ceremonial Festival** - First Sunday of September (Eternal Kagura Offering Festival)

